  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana to check how it renders. The create calls were accepted, and the rule's first run was still pending when I stopped.\n\n**Data**: the Kibana Sample Data Logs view (`kibana_sample_data_logs`) holds web access logs. The fields I used are `timestamp`, `response` (a string, so I query `response.keyword`), `bytes`, `clientip`, `extension`, `geo.*`, `machine.os` and `url`.\n\n**Dashboard**: \"EFF-1f401d9c Web logs overview\" (id `aad42ec8-89ce-47fb-9be5-15b9c6c14729`). It defaults to the last 7 days and has five panels:\n- **Total requests**: a count metric.\n- **Average bytes**: the mean of `bytes`.\n- **Unique clients**: a distinct count of `clientip`.\n- **Requests over time**: a line chart of request counts by time.\n- **Requests by response code**: a bar chart of the top 10 response codes.\n\n**Rule**: \"EFF-1f401d9c 5xx spike\" (id `bbd6bd52-ecc6-4418-8ec7-1db7d5d4bfbd`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over a 5-minute window.\n- It fires when that count is above 10.\n- It has no actions attached, so a firing only shows up in Kibana's alert list and notifies nothing. You didn't ask for notifications.",
